      "category": "interface-cannot-represent",
      "reason": "The kernel truncates records written to /dev/kmsg to about 1 KiB, so no record near the 8192-byte read size can exist on the live ring; that boundary is exercised only through the fixture-file path (legacy/dmesg/split-read-boundary)"
    },
    {
      "topic": "doas/authority_policy_and_verification",
      "category": "kernel-authority-absent",
      "reason": "The observable is produced by the real /dev/bashos-auth kernel authority (v2 allow, policy deny, non-interactive denial and verifier outcomes are decided by it); no guest kernel provides that device and a synthetic stand-in would not be the authority's answer."
    },
    {
      "topic": "login/credential-transition-deferred",
      "category": "kernel-authority-absent",
      "reason": "Token acceptance (--auth-fd CONSUME, --auth-token) is decided by the real /dev/bashos-auth kernel authority (BL_AUTH_IOC_MINT/CONSUME); no guest kernel provides that device and a synthetic stand-in would not be the authority's answer."
    },
    {
      "topic": "login/interactive-auth-session-deferred",
      "category": "kernel-authority-absent",
      "reason": "A successful login needs the real /dev/bashos-auth kernel proof before session accounting and the shell exec; no guest kernel provides that device and a synthetic stand-in would not be the authority's answer."
    },
    {
      "topic": "sshd/authentication-authorization",
      "category": "kernel-authority-absent",
      "reason": "The password half of PermitRootLogin prohibit-password versus yes is decided by the /dev/bashos-auth kernel authority (BSSHD_AUTH_DEV in sshd.c), which the disposable guest does not provide; without it no password attempt can be told apart from a refusal, and a stand-in authority would not test the real one. Admitted root command execution is covered by the privilege=root case."
    },
    {
      "topic": "sudo/authority-command-authorization",
      "category": "kernel-authority-absent",
      "reason": "The observable is produced by the real /dev/bashos-auth kernel authority (command authorization, policy denial and reauthorization are decided by it); no guest kernel provides that device and a synthetic stand-in would not be the authority's answer."
    },
    {
      "topic": "sudo/sudoedit-safe-copyback",
      "category": "kernel-authority-absent",
      "reason": "The observable is produced by the real /dev/bashos-auth kernel authority (kernel safe-open of policy-bound targets and digest-bound copyback); no guest kernel provides that device and a synthetic stand-in would not be the authority's answer."
    },
